# Technical Documentation: Backend Length Validation Sweep & System Integrity Fixes

## 1. Overview & Goal

The primary objective of this engineering sweep was to establish strict **backend length validation (`min_length` and `max_length`)** across all API schemas and routers supporting **LEA (Law Enforcement Agency)** and **FDA (Food and Drug Administration)** pages in the E-VerifyMo Desktop application.

While frontend input constraints (e.g., `maxLength`, `minLength` on React `<input>` and `<textarea>` elements) provide user experience feedback, they do not constitute a security boundary. Any direct HTTP API call (via Postman, cURL, or custom scripts) can bypass frontend attributes. Establishing matching backend limits guarantees data integrity, prevents database truncation errors, and protects PostgreSQL columns.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Scope Boundary Enforcement**:
> - All `.jsx` frontend files remained completely untouched.
> - Draft schemas (`*DraftSave`) enforce maximum length limits only—they never enforce `min_length` constraints to allow partial drafts.
> - Search and filter parameters enforce maximum length limits without minimum length restrictions.
> - Dropdown-backed filter fields (such as `category` and `reason_closed`) were exempted from hardcoded `max_length` string constraints to allow for future Enum refactoring.

